Helios DXVK fork: hand-off waits bounded by the device's next own point

Conduit guest/windows/docs/shared-surfaces.md section 4. Applied after 0008
by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

The ledger is sampled on the CS thread, which can run well behind the
application thread. A lagging CS thread then samples another process's
points that were published after this device's own next hand-off point, i.e.
after the work being recorded was issued, so that work cannot depend on
them. Waiting for such a point closes a cycle: the other process's
submission that signals it waits for our point, which rides a later
submission of ours that our worker never reaches. Only the two-second wait
cap broke it (keyed-load perf mode: two seconds per round, which looked like a
hang).

- HeliosHandoffHooks::sampleBefore (appended): as sampleAll, given the
  DxvkDevice, so the UMD returns only points published before the device's
  next own point its CS thread has not reached. Points are a ledger-wide
  sequence, so they compare across devices.
- HeliosHandoffHooks::csReached (appended) and
  D3D11ImmediateContext::HeliosSignalHandoffPoint: the CS thread reports
  reaching the signal of an own hand-off point.

